meta {
  name: But a domain C user token DOES open Application C
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

get {
  url: {{appC}}/api/inventory
  body: none
  auth: oauth2
}

auth:oauth2 {
  grant_type: password
  access_token_url: {{tokenUrlC}}
  username: {{frankUser}}
  password: {{frankPassword}}
  client_id: {{clientCTestCli}}
  client_secret: {{clientCTestCliSecret}}
  scope: openid profile email
  credentials_placement: body
  credentials_id: domainC-frank
  token_placement: header
  token_header_prefix: Bearer
  auto_fetch_token: true
  auto_refresh_token: true
}

assert {
  $res.status: 200
}

tests {
  test("a user of THIS domain is accepted", function() {
    const caller = res.getBody().caller;
    expect(caller.username).to.equal("frank");
    expect(caller.serviceAccount).to.equal(false);
  });

  test("issued by domain C, not domain A", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().caller.issuer)
      .to.equal("http://localhost:8090/realms/sso-domain-c");
  });

  test("frank holds domain C's role", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().caller.roles).to.include("app-c-api-reader");
  });
}

docs {
  The other half of the isolation proof, and the reason this file sits in 04-negative next to
  app-c-with-domain-a-token.bru rather than in a folder of its own. Read the two together:

    app-c-with-domain-a-token.bru   domain A machine token -> Application C  ->  401
    app-c-inventory-as-frank.bru    domain C user token    -> Application C  ->  200

  Without this one, a 401 in the other file proves very little - Application C might simply be
  broken, or rejecting everything. Together they show the rejection is specifically about WHICH
  identity provider vouched for the caller.

  frank exists only in SSO domain C. He is not a user of domain A, and alice is not a user of this
  domain; there is no federation between them. Completes the pair checked by
  .\scripts\get-token.ps1 -Scenario cross-domain-rejected

  domain-c-test-cli is a lab-only client with the direct access grant enabled, so a domain C user
  token can be fetched without a browser. See ../../../keycloak/domain-c/README.md.
}
